[#:seon.task{:name
             "recording-an-exit-never-parks-under-the-process-mutex",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (e4_osproc E4.2): with 10 and 30 children reaped from fibers at once, 1 and 3 .waitFor calls threw 'jolt-lock-wait: a fiber cannot leave the CPU while its carrier holds a counted lock (1 held)'; that fiber died and the exit status was lost (an :unknown exit). Trace: proc-wait-blocking -> proc-reap-once (jolt-with-mutex proc-p-mutex, process.ss:2184) -> proc-record-exit! (2174) -> proc-shut-stdin!/proc-shut-idle-output! (962-974) -> fd shutdown -> forget! -> monitor-enter-slow!, which parks while the counted mutex is held. Correct: under the mutex only commit the exit-box; close the pipes after releasing it. By design (process.ss:2179, 2218), waitFor is a WNOHANG poll backing off 1->10 ms; the event is the child's pipe EOF (IO poller), after which the reap answers at once (median 14 us after EOF, e4).",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{jolt.process-test/thirty-children-reaped-from-fibers-all-have-their-status}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-fiber-dump-follows-the-redirected-stderr"
  :seon.task/plan "A forked Seon agent redirects its stderr to a file of its own (```seon.agent/stderr-to-file!```: creat, then dup2 onto fd 2), so a crash or a stall dump can be read from <agents-dir>/<id>.err (```seon.fx/stall-evidence``` sends SIGINFO, then reads that file into fibers.txt). The fiber dump a Jolt process writes on SIGINFO did not follow: in the source-test hang of 2026-10-27 the stuck agents' dumps (fiber 0 parked in seon.sock/recv! under seon.agent/prove!) were found in the root's own runs/root.log, and the agent's .err stayed 0 bytes, fibers.txt empty. Jolt writes the dump to the stderr port it opened at start (the inherited descriptor's buffer or a dup of it), not to fd 2 by number. Make the dump write fd 2 at the time it is written (or reopen its port after a dup2), with a test that dup2s a file over fd 2 in a child, sends SIGINFO and reads the file. Evidence: the shared notes folder, hang-source-test-nextgen-2026-10-27/diagnosis.md (root.log.full lines 1-92 against runs/store-agents/*/a2.err)."
  :seon.task/kind :whole
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-childs-exit-is-a-channel-value"
  :seon.task/plan "Seon waits for a socket's input or a child's exit with C of its own (seon.lib/kq-wait, native e13_wait, kqueue's NOTE_EXIT: macOS only), in the fork server's loop (seon.fork-server) and seon.agent/read-to-exit. The owner, 2026-10-30: one common interface on every platform, the platform's difference in Jolt. Correct: Jolt answers a child's exit as a value on a channel (kqueue's NOTE_EXIT on macOS, a pidfd in epoll on Linux), beside jolt.io-poller's readiness, so a caller waits with alts! over the exit and its input; Seon's e13_wait and kq-wait are deleted. Its gates on macOS and in an arm64 Linux container (Docker)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.process-test/a-childs-exit-arrives-on-a-channel}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-hosts-memory-and-signals-are-jolts"
  :seon.task/plan "Seon reads its own memory through macOS's task_info (seon.lib/real-mb, phys_footprint) and writes the host's signal numbers itself (seon.lib/SIGUSR1, seon.lib/SIGIO: 30 and 23 on macOS, 10 and 29 on Linux). The owner, 2026-10-30: no platform checks outside Jolt. Correct: jolt.host answers this process's memory as the platform charges it (phys_footprint on macOS, the proportional set from /proc/self/smaps_rollup on Linux, each docstring naming what it measures) and the signal numbers by name; Seon's copies are deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.process-test/a-process-reads-its-own-footprint}}]
